      "jurisdiction": "Vermont state courts",
      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "Toensing v. Attorney Gen. of Vt.",
          "citation": "178 A.3d 1000 (Vt. 2017)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Vermont",
          "year": 2017,
          "date_decided": "2017-10-20",
          "holding": "Records produced or acquired in the course of agency business are public records under the Public Records Act even when they sit in employees' private accounts, and where a requester specifically seeks such communications the agency must ask the named employees to produce them.",
          "relevance": "Toensing is the controlling Vermont analogue to FOIA for records requests directed at state child-welfare agencies.",
          "quote": "the PRA's definition of \"public record\" includes digital documents stored in private accounts, but emphasize that it extends only to documents that otherwise meet the definition of public records",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6483671/toensing-v-attorney-gen-of-vt/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 6483671
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re Appeal of H.H.",
          "citation": "2020 VT 107",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Vermont",
          "year": 2020,
          "date_decided": "2020-12-31",
          "holding": "Summary judgment applying collateral estoppel to a registry substantiation was reversed because the Human Services Board could not inspect the related CHINS file without a need-to-know designation from the family court under 33 V.S.A. § 5117(b)(1)(F), and the stipulated facts did not establish what the substantiation required.",
          "relevance": "H.H. is the Vermont authority on the need-to-know designation required before juvenile-case records may be inspected outside the case.",
          "quote": "A need-to-know designation from the family court was required before the Board could inspect the contents of the juvenile files in question.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4844385/in-re-appeal-of-hh/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4844385
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re A.O. & I.O., Juveniles & In re B.G. & E.G., Juveniles",
          "citation": "2023 VT 54",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Vermont",
          "year": 2023,
          "date_decided": "2023-09-29",
          "holding": "The CHINS merits and disposition orders for four children were reversed and vacated as unsupported by the findings or the record, and the orders allowing a party's advocate to inspect the other children's juvenile records without a need-to-know designation were reversed.",
          "relevance": "It marks the statutory limits on disclosure of juvenile-case records under 33 V.S.A. § 5117, which bounds any transparency request.",
          "quote": "We therefore reverse the CHINS merits determination as unsupported by the findings or the record.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10284039/in-re-ao-io-juveniles-in-re-bg-eg-juveniles/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 10284039
        }
